The two vectors are drawn **tail-to-tail**, so the angle marked is the one at their common starting point.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta, angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (3.6,1.2);
\coordinate (B) at (1.4,3.0);
\draw[-{Stealth[length=3mm]}] (O) -- (A) node[right] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[-{Stealth[length=3mm]}] (O) -- (B) node[above] {$\underset{\sim}{b}$};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.8cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = A--O--B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
1. **Find the dot product of the two vectors**
$\underset{\sim}{a}\cdot\underset{\sim}{b} = (4)(-2)+(-1)(5)$
$= -13$
2. **Find the magnitude of each vector**
$|\underset{\sim}{a}| = \sqrt{4^2+(-1)^2} = \sqrt{17}$
$|\underset{\sim}{b}| = \sqrt{(-2)^2+5^2} = \sqrt{29}$
3. **Substitute into the cosine formula and take the inverse cosine**
$\cos\theta = \dfrac{-13}{\sqrt{17}\times\sqrt{29}}$
$\theta = \cos^{-1}(-0.5854\ldots)$
$\approx 126^{\circ}$
The cosine is negative, so the angle is obtuse.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta, angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (2,-0.5);
\coordinate (B) at (-1,2.5);
\draw[-{Stealth[length=3mm]}] (O) -- (A) node[pos=1, right] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\draw[-{Stealth[length=3mm]}] (O) -- (B) node[pos=1, above] {$\underset{\sim}{b}$};
\pic[draw, "$126^{\circ}$", angle radius=0.7cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = A--O--B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
